{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli succeeded as Count of Tripoli in 1152 following the death of his father Pons. He was the son of Pons and his wife Cecilia who belonged to the House of Toulouse. Raymond III was born around the year 1115 and through his mother he inherited rights to the county of Tripoli from her family. His reign marked significant developments in the Crusader States particularly within the County of Tripoli.\n\n*Highlighted section part 1: Early Life and Accession*\nRaymond III was educated in Antioch and his upbringing was influenced by the cultural and political environment of the Principality of Antioch. Before ascending to the throne Raymond spent time at the court of his uncle Bohemond II. His early life was marked by the political intrigues of the Crusader States where alliances and rivalries often dictated the fate of individuals. When Pons died in 1152 Raymond inherited the County of Tripoli without immediate challenges to his authority though he faced the complexities of governing a region affected by ongoing conflicts with Muslim forces and internal power struggles.\n\nDuring his early years as count Raymond III focused on consolidating his power and securing the borders of his domain. One of his first major actions was to negotiate a truce with Nur ad-Din a powerful Zengid ruler. This diplomatic effort aimed to provide a temporary respite allowing Raymond to strengthen his position internally. The truce however did not last long and Raymond found himself embroiled in conflicts that defined his reign.\n\n*Highlighted section part 2: Military Campaigns and Strategic Alliances*\nRaymond III was known for his military prowess and his ability to form strategic alliances. In 1157 he married Hodierna of Jerusalem the daughter of Queen Melisende and King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. This marriage significantly strengthened his ties to the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Hodierna brought substantial dowry lands to the union which Raymond used to fortify the borders of Tripoli against potential invasions.\n\nOne of Raymond’s notable military engagements occurred in 1164 when he led a joint Crusader force alongside Baldwin III of Jerusalem and Amalric I of Jerusalem. Their combined armies aimed to capture the city of Harenc a strategic stronghold held by Nur ad-Din. Despite initial successes the Crusaders ultimately failed to take the city. This campaign highlighted the challenges faced by the Crusader States in their efforts to maintain and expand their territories against well-organized Muslim opposition.\n\nRaymond’s diplomatic skills were also evident in his interactions with the Byzantine Empire. He maintained a complex relationship with the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Komnenos often balancing between cooperation and conflict. Raymond’s strategic maneuvers included forming alliances with other Crusader leaders to counter Byzantine influence in the region.\n\n*Highlighted section part 3: Later Years and Legacy*\nIn the later years of his reign Raymond III faced increasing pressure from Nur ad-Din who sought to expand his control over the region. Raymond’s efforts to hold onto Tripoli were complicated by internal dissensions and the broader geopolitical shifts in the area. His relationships with other Crusader leaders such as the rulers of Antioch and Jerusalem were often fraught with tension and mistrust.\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 his legacy marked by both military achievements and diplomatic complexities. His reign saw significant attempts to defend and expand the County of Tripoli in the face of formidable adversaries. He was succeeded by his son Raymond IV who inherited a county that was still vulnerable to external threats. Raymond III’s life and rule reflect the broader struggles of the Crusader States during the 12th century where survival and adaptation were constant challenges.\n\nHistori